\p{\strong{Status:} accepted. This is the instruction-set document in full: revision 2, with the notes added on 2026-11-24 while the machine was built (the last paragraph of 1.3, the decision in 8.2, 13.7 and the last rows of section 15). The [reconciliation of the design review](design-reconciliation-document) has authority over it, and the errata of its section 5 correct it.}

\p{Revision 2. It applies \code{\verb~|OWNER-NOTES.md~} (notes 1-10) and \code{\verb~|RECONCILIATION.md~} (B1-B4, sections 2-4) to the first draft; the change log (section 15) maps every change to the finding it answers. Order of authority: \code{\verb~|OWNER-NOTES.md~} > \code{\verb~|RECONCILIATION.md~} > this document > research documents.}

\p{Scope: everything that happens inside one worker while it checks declarations: machine state, values, code objects, instructions, memoisation, tiers, and the module that implements them (\code{\verb~|rlvm-machine~}, section 13). The term store, loader, declaration table, admission pipeline, reference kernel, inductive checker, scheduler and packaging belong to \code{\verb~|ARCHITECTURE.md~}; section 12 lists what this document requires from that side and section 14 is the facade through which that side uses the machine.}

\p{References: \code{\verb~|R02 §x~} = \src{docs/research/02-kernel-typechecker.md} section x (the official rule; C++ line numbers are given there), likewise \code{\verb~|R01~}..\code{\verb~|R13~}; \code{\verb~|R13~} (\src{docs/research/13-workload-characterization.md}) is the dynamic profile of the Cedar-level corpora, taken with an instrumented nano-lean and counted in instructions. Evidence tags: \strong{\verb~|[~meas\verb~|]~} measured on this host (\code{\verb~|spikes/machine/results/~}, file named), \strong{\verb~|[~R13\verb~|]~} measured there, \strong{\verb~|[~src\verb~|]~} read in a leader's source via the research documents, \strong{\verb~|[~est\verb~|]~} an estimate with the arithmetic shown. Export data used in the examples was dumped from \code{\verb~|work/arena-site/tests/good/init-prelude.ndjson~} and \code{\verb~|generated/init.ndjson~}.}

\p{Notation: \code{\verb~|T~}/\code{\verb~|F~}/\code{\verb~|U~} = true / false / undecided (budget ran out); \code{\verb~|I~} = one hash-consing probe; \code{\verb~|P~} = push + pop + dispatch of one worklist frame; \code{\verb~|D~} = fetch + decode of one code instruction. Section 0.3 gives what was measured for them. The cost columns of sections 4 and 7 are \strong{instruction estimates} made with \code{\verb~|I~} = 120, \code{\verb~|P~} = 25, \code{\verb~|D~} = 12; they rank alternatives inside one mechanism and are not CPU-time predictions (0.3 shows a factor of 8 between instructions per nanosecond on a cached and on a 116 MB working set).}

\subtree{
\title{0. Decisions in one page}

\subtree{
\title{0.1 Metric and yardstick}

\ul{
\li{\strong{CPU time} (task-clock summed over all threads, compiler and collector threads included) is the primary metric; wall clock with parallel workers is the second; peak RSS the third. Instruction counts are recorded because the arena's board uses them; they are not optimised. Every table lists CPU time, wall clock, RSS, instructions, in that order, where the source has them.}
\li{Targets, measured on this host under the arena's limits (RECONCILIATION §0): mathlib gruszka 62.4 s CPU / 18.1 s wall / 2.53 GB, nano-lean 85.6 s / 25.5 s, sokonanoda 101.4 s / 30.5 s; Init gruszka 2.8 s CPU; Std 5.7 s CPU. Machine gates on Init: at most 7.0 s CPU (2.5x) after staging step 1 and 4.2 s (1.5x) after step 4 (section 11).}
\li{\strong{Cedar-level corpora} (cedar, con-leche, reachability, navier-stokes-euler, cslib, then mathlib) are the yardstick for every tiering, heat and compilation decision; Init and Std are regression corpora. The fast benchmarks are \code{\verb~|work/workloads/slices/cedar-hot.ndjson~} (the cone of cedar's 172 most expensive own declarations: inference and conversion) and \code{\verb~|work/exports/reachability.ndjson~} (closed evaluation), with \code{\verb~|perf/unfold-before-literal-bad~} as the depth and Nat-literal probe \verb~|[~R13 §6\verb~|]~. What \code{\verb~|R13~} found (instrumented nano-lean, one thread; \strong{instruction counts}: no CPU-time profile of these corpora exists yet, the first machine gate supplies it):}
}

\table{
\tr{\th{\verb~|[~R13 §3\verb~|]~}\th{cedar}\th{reachability}\th{con-leche}\th{navier-stokes-euler}}
\tr{\td{declarations; instructions}\td{151,772; 64.1 G}\td{4,846; 11.1 G}\td{38,246; 61.9 G}\td{129,845; 103 G}}
\tr{\td{phase shares: inference / reduction / conversion / import}\td{42 / 25 / 20 / 9 \%}\td{4 / 93 / 1 / 1 \%}\td{38 / 31 / 21 / 7 \%}\td{54 / 16 / 19 / 9 \%}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|infer~} calls (misses)}\td{42.0 M (13.7 M)}\td{0.71 M (0.22 M)}\td{39.4 M (11.3 M)}\td{77.5 M (19.1 M)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|eval~} calls (misses)}\td{94.6 M (50.3 M)}\td{11.0 M (6.5 M)}\td{94.3 M (46.4 M)}\td{195 M (91.4 M)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|def_eq~} calls (not answered by identity or the pair cache)}\td{36.2 M (5.66 M)}\td{0.55 M (0.06 M)}\td{33.9 M (4.05 M)}\td{85.5 M (11.5 M)}}
\tr{\td{delta / derived-rule firings / iota / projection of a constructor / Nat operations}\td{2.77 M / 0.69 M / 0.86 M / 1.06 M / 0.12 M}\td{0.14 M / 0.89 M / 1.05 M / 0.50 M / 0.07 M}\td{2.79 M / 0.56 M / 1.45 M / 0.95 M / 0.08 M}\td{3.82 M / 0.58 M / 0.28 M / 2.03 M / 0.06 M}}
\tr{\td{values made}\td{34.8 M}\td{6.2 M}\td{28.1 M}\td{53.0 M}}
\tr{\td{declarations without any reduction step (their share of the instructions)}\td{57.5\% (9.0\%)}\td{64.0\% (1.5\%)}\td{47.6\% (4.9\%)}\td{42.0\% (3.6\%)}}
\tr{\td{declarations with 10^4 reduction steps or more (their share)}\td{32 (5.9\%)}\td{6 (93.4\%)}\td{44 (18.5\%)}\td{11 (7.7\%)}}
\tr{\td{code objects (bodies + rules + projections + telescopes) executed 10^4 / 10^5 / 10^6 times or more}\td{175 / 23 / 0}\td{39 / 10 / 0}\td{176 / 22 / 0}\td{307 / 18 / 0}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean's derived rules off against on}\td{1.04x}\td{1.89x}\td{1.05x}\td{1.00x}}
}

\p{Further \verb~|[~R13 §0, §3.1, §5\verb~|]~: by what the code does, cedar spends 33\% of its instructions walking terms and environments, 21\% building and interning values (+4\% in the hash tables), 18\% in inference proper, 7\% in conversion proper; 12.9\% is evaluation \em{under inference}, the work of \code{\verb~|TELE~}. Cedar's own declarations are less reduction-heavy than Init's (17\% against 38\% of the instructions) and larger (1.02 M against 0.38 M instructions each). 93.6\% of cedar's unfoldings are of constants declared in Init; by role \code{\verb~|casesOn~} 21.5\%, structure projection functions 19.8\%, matchers 14.2\%. On mathlib 1,073 / 129 / 1 objects reach 10^4 / 10^5 / 10^6 executions; the one above 10^6 is the telescope of \code{\verb~|Eq~} (3.1 M); no body, rule or projection reaches 10^6 in any corpus.}

\p{Reading: at this level the checker mostly \em{walks typed terms} and makes values; reduction is a thin, heavy tail of declarations and one corpus (reachability). Consequences: (a) the roles that specialise \em{inference and conversion} to a constant (\code{\verb~|TELE~}, \code{\verb~|CONG~}), matching instead of building, and the session layout carry the common case; hash-consing, a quarter of the work, is removed by no code tier; (b) the reduction roles carry the tail, and three shapes (\code{\verb~|PROJARG~}, \code{\verb~|CASESON~}, \code{\verb~|MATCHER~}) cover 55\% of cedar's unfoldings; (c) derived rules matter on closed evaluation only; (d) compiled code has no target on the library corpora (0.5).}
}

\subtree{
\title{0.2 Decisions}

\ol{
\li{\strong{The machine is a worklist machine} in the sense of Zhao, Oliveira, Schrijvers (ICFP 2019). Their worklist is \code{\verb~|Γ ::= · | Γ, a | Γ, x:A | Γ ⊩ ω~} with judgment chains \code{\verb~|ω ::= A ≤ B | e ⇐ A | e ⇒_a ω | A • e ⇒⇒_a ω~}; an inference judgment carries its continuation \code{\verb~|ω~} and binds its result \code{\verb~|a~} in it; a variable is dropped when it reaches the top; the algorithm is \code{\verb~|Γ ⟶ Γ'~} and succeeds when \code{\verb~|Γ ⟶* ·~}. RLVM: a stack \code{\verb~|W~} of frames; every frame is a judgment waiting for the result of the frame above it; the result is bound through register \code{\verb~|A~}; binders are entries of the same order; a declaration is accepted iff \code{\verb~|W~} reduces to its bottom sentinel without \code{\verb~|FAIL~}. This transition system is the \strong{specification} (1.3). How the host executes it (a loop over materialised frames, or bounded host recursion that keeps the top frames in Java locals) is a separate, measured choice (8.2).}
\li{\strong{The guest language is the judgment language.} Terms and values are data. A constant is elaborated into the \em{residual judgment chains obtained by specialising the kernel's judgments to that constant}. The primary roles are the judgment-specialised ones: \code{\verb~|TELE(c)~} (application inference \code{\verb~|c • ā ⇒⇒ T~}), \code{\verb~|CONG(c)~} (conversion \code{\verb~|c ā ≡ c b̄~}: a per-position comparison plan, 6.1) and \code{\verb~|RULES(c)~} (weak head normalisation \code{\verb~|c ā ⇝ v~} by case analysis on the arguments: the casesOn and matcher shapes in K1, derived trees in K2). \code{\verb~|BODY(c)~} / \code{\verb~|RULE(r, j)~} are the general form of the reduction role, and \code{\verb~|FACTS(c)~} is the record of classification facts all of them read.}
\li{\strong{Run-once terms are not elaborated.} A proof or a declaration type is walked from the term table by generic judgments (\code{\verb~|EVAL~}, \code{\verb~|INFER~}, \code{\verb~|CHECK~}), which are the unspecialised form of the same chains. A role is elaborated rent-to-own, at about its 10th use (3.1): elaboration costs 3-4 µs per telescope and pays back after 22-35 uses \verb~|[~meas\verb~|]~.}
\li{\strong{Values} are 32-bit refs: small Nat immediates (31 bits) or indices of 32-byte records in a flat \code{\verb~|int[]~}. Kinds: SORT, NBIG, STR, LAM, PI, PII, SUSP, FVAR, CONST, NEU, PROJ, NSUC; kinds 13-15 are reserved for packed representations (owner note 8). Every record is hash-consed; four memo words live in the record.}
\li{\strong{A neutral is \code{\verb~|(head, run)~}}, a closure is \code{\verb~|(binder term, frame run, ls)~}, a suspension is \code{\verb~|(term, frame run, ls)~}, where \code{\verb~|ls~} is a \strong{level-substitution id} \code{\verb~|mkSubst(lparams(row), levels)~} (2.7). A \em{run} is an immutable, interned sequence of value refs (spines and captured frames); working environments live in a separate non-interned slab.}
\li{\strong{Closure identity is tier-independent}: \code{\verb~|(lambda term id, frame pruned by the node's mask, ls)~}. Generic walk and elaborated code must produce \em{the same value ids}; this is a test invariant.}
\li{\strong{Constants stay folded}; \code{\verb~|EVAL~} does beta, zeta and projection-of-constructor only; delta is the \code{\verb~|DELTA~} judgment, memoised on the value, driven by \code{\verb~|WHNF~} and lazy delta in the official order.}
\li{\strong{Arguments}: never evaluated when the callee's binder is unread; evaluated at once when inert (cannot beta/zeta/project); delayed as a hash-consed suspension when active.}
\li{\strong{Free variables} are \code{\verb~|FVAR(type, k)~}: a binder takes the first variable of its type that is not in scope, so a re-opened binder reproduces every id below it (gruszka's \code{\verb~|pick_var~}, R05 §2.9).}
\li{\strong{The complete procedure is the official one} (R02 §6-8) transcribed to values, state for state. Every shortcut is either a fact derived from the store (3.5) or a speculative segment under a \code{\verb~|TRY~} frame whose three outcomes are \code{\verb~|T~}, \code{\verb~|F~}, \code{\verb~|U~}; \code{\verb~|U~} and any \em{recoverable} failure raised inside the segment fall through to the complete procedure; a \em{fatal} fault (an invariant seen broken, a cancellation, the memory watermark) ends the declaration with DECLINE whatever is on \code{\verb~|W~} (1.4). Nothing computed under a budget that ran out enters a memo.}
\li{\strong{Tabling}: \code{\verb~|EVAL~}, \code{\verb~|INFER~} and \code{\verb~|CHECK~} are memoised on the SUSP record of \code{\verb~|(term, pruned frame, ls)~}; \code{\verb~|WHNF~}, \code{\verb~|CORE~}, \code{\verb~|DELTA~}, \code{\verb~|VTYPE~} in value slots; \code{\verb~|CONV~} in one pair table with status bits. Scope is the session; check-mode results carry a stamp (5.3).}
\li{\strong{Nat is specialised in the ISA} (owner note 7): immediates, \code{\verb~|NSUC~} offset cells that fold to a literal exactly when the official \code{\verb~|whnf~} does, \code{\verb~|NATOP~} with an exact overflow guard, constructor views of literals for iota without allocation.}
\li{\strong{Static facts} are one immutable record per constant, derived lazily by any worker from the store alone, published by a single store of its index. A sort level is recorded only when it is manifest (3.5). Everything visible to more than one session obeys the \strong{publication rule} (3.3): only export or generated term ids, constant ids, name ids and base level ids.}
\li{\strong{Sessions} are small: the footprint (bytes in use, counted once) is capped at 16 MiB by default, reset costs O(used), and the layout keeps a probe to one or two cache lines (1.6).}
\li{\strong{Tiers}: K0 = generic judgments written in Java; K1 = code objects executed by the plain-Java code stepper, all calling one operation library \code{\verb~|Ops~}. K2 = derived case trees, the second level of the primary role \code{\verb~|RULES~}: designed (3.6), \strong{off} until its soundness conditions hold. T2 = Truffle compilation of hot code objects: an \strong{experiment}, off until the go/no-go of 0.5 is met. Nothing in K0 or K1 is shaped for the sake of either.}
\li{\strong{Limits produce DECLINE, never ACCEPT}; a REJECT is returned to the driver for confirmation by the reference kernel; the Nat-size guards reject as the official kernel does (section 9).}
\li{\strong{Strict mode} (the cold re-check): generic tier only, the complete procedure, only the exact shortcuts, a fresh session, no shared facts or code (section 14).}
\li{\strong{Reuse verdict}: no reference-count or uniqueness bits on values. In-place mutation exists where ownership is structural: worklist frames, scratch buffers, the top of the environment slab, memo slots (section 10).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{0.3 What the spikes measured}

\p{\code{\verb~|spikes/machine/results/~} (aarch64, container with the arena's limits; \code{\verb~|nce~} = native image of GraalVM CE 25, \code{\verb~|jvm~} = HotSpot with the Graal JIT, Serial GC; 2 runs on \code{\verb~|nce~}, 3 on \code{\verb~|jvm~}; ranges are over the runs). "Pass" is the single-threaded elapsed time of the measured loop, i.e. the worker's CPU time.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Quantity}\th{CPU time}\th{Instructions}\th{File}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|I~}, hit: \code{\verb~|mkApp~} of an existing value, 4096 values, everything cached}\td{3.8 ns (\code{\verb~|nce~}), 2.9 ns (\code{\verb~|jvm~}); probe hand-inlined with the arrays in locals: 2.8 ns (\code{\verb~|nce~})}\td{110 (\code{\verb~|nce~}), 38 (\code{\verb~|jvm~}); hand-inlined 45}\td{\code{\verb~|micro.txt~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|I~}, miss: \code{\verb~|mkApp~} of a new value into a growing arena}\td{55 ns (\code{\verb~|nce~})}\td{156}\td{\code{\verb~|micro.txt~}}}
\tr{\td{Telescope instantiation, whole Init: 2,088,996 constant-headed spines, 4.2 M values made, 64 MB table + 52 MB arena. First pass}\td{generic walk with closed-term memo 810-904 ms; code objects 756-842 ms including 115-126 ms to elaborate all 39,783 used constants}\td{4.41 G; 3.20 G of which elaboration 0.62 G}\td{\code{\verb~|exp1-nce.txt~}}}
\tr{\td{The same, passes 2-3 (every probe hits; the working set stays 116 MB)}\td{generic 910-1079 ms; code objects 689-705 ms}\td{2030 and 1167 per spine}\td{\code{\verb~|exp1-nce.txt~} (\code{\verb~|p3~})}}
\tr{\td{The same on Std: 3,115,533 spines}\td{generic 1621-1759 ms; code objects 1334-1463 ms including 281 ms of elaboration (69,712 constants)}\td{11.9 G; 5.7 G of which elaboration 1.53 G}\td{\code{\verb~|exp4-std-nce.txt~}}}
\tr{\td{Match mode: domains matched against existing values, only the result type built}\td{526-599 ms against 689-705 ms for building (\code{\verb~|nce~}); 509-513 against 633-650 ms (\code{\verb~|jvm~}, steady)}\td{1124 against 1167 per spine (\code{\verb~|nce~})}\td{\code{\verb~|exp1-*.txt~} (\code{\verb~|bm~}), \code{\verb~|steady-jvm.txt~}}}
\tr{\td{Elaboration of one telescope}\td{3.0 µs (Init), 4.0 µs (Std)}\td{15.6 k, 21.9 k (about 220 per code word)}\td{\code{\verb~|exp1-nce.txt~}, \code{\verb~|exp4-std-nce.txt~}}}
\tr{\td{Saving of a code object per use}\td{86 ns (Init, first pass), 133 ns (Init, steady), 184 ns (Std)}\td{880 (Init), 2,400 (Std)}\td{derived from the rows above}}
\tr{\td{Worklist against host recursion: eval + readback of the 59,979 declaration types of Init, hash-consing on}\td{recursive 399-464 ms; worklist \code{\verb~|wl~} 405-501 ms; worklist \code{\verb~|wl2~} 331-412 ms: the difference is inside the run-to-run spread (\code{\verb~|nce~}, 2 runs)}\td{\code{\verb~|wl~} +32\%, \code{\verb~|wl2~} +18\% (\code{\verb~|nce~}); +31\%, +39\% (\code{\verb~|jvm~}, steady)}\td{\code{\verb~|exp2.txt~}, \code{\verb~|tables.md~}}}
\tr{\td{The same with hash-consing off (bump allocation)}\td{recursive 70-78 ms; \code{\verb~|wl~} 85-89 ms (+18\%); \code{\verb~|wl2~} 77-81 ms (+7\%)}\td{+51\%, +29\%}\td{\code{\verb~|exp2.txt~}}}
\tr{\td{Truffle-compiled telescope roots, whole Init, one pass, compilation in the background}\td{none: 1.04 s CPU / 1.07 s wall; 184 roots call-threaded: 1.63 s / 1.06 s; 184 roots with probes inlined: 4.57 s / 1.65 s; 33 roots: 1.17 s / 1.07 s and 1.80 s / 1.00 s}\td{6.49 G; 15.1 G; 68.9 G; 8.26 G; 21.7 G}\td{\code{\verb~|exp1-nce.txt~}}}
\tr{\td{Hot subset (184 constants, 1,619,861 spines), pass in steady state}\td{code stepper 413-422 ms; compiled call-threaded 400-414 ms; compiled with probes inlined 323-362 ms. Compilation: 3.5 ms CPU per root call-threaded, 18 ms with probes inlined}\td{1023, 865, 605 per spine; 47 M and 327 M per root}\td{\code{\verb~|hot-nce.txt~}}}
\tr{\td{Heat: share of instantiations that happen after a constant crossed a threshold of 10 uses}\td{Init 93.8\% with 6,002 of 39,783 used constants above it; Std 93.0\% with 9,875 of 69,712}\td{}\td{\code{\verb~|heat-init.txt~}, \code{\verb~|heat-std.txt~}}}
}

\p{Three readings that the rest of the document relies on:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Memory traffic, not instruction count, sets the CPU time.} The same probe code runs at 29 instructions per ns on a cached table and at 3.5 per ns in the whole-Init pass (1167 instructions in 335 ns per spine), where every probe hits but the working set is 116 MB \verb~|[~meas; the attribution to cache and TLB misses is an inference\verb~|]~. Hence small sessions, one-line probes and matching instead of building (owner note 10); hence also the 16 MiB default of 1.6.}
\li{\strong{Matching beats building by 15-25\% of the pass time at equal instruction counts} \verb~|[~meas\verb~|]~.}
\li{\strong{The worklist costs instructions, and so far no measurable CPU time when values are hash-consed.} The spike's machine is the best case (one method, registers in locals, frames of 2-3 words); frames of the shape of 1.3 are measured by \code{\verb~|spikes/stepper~} before any judgment is written (8.2).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{0.4 What following the vision costs}

\table{
\tr{\th{Vision item}\th{Research recommendation}\th{Cost}\th{What it buys}}
\tr{\td{judgments on a worklist, small-step}\td{host recursion + hand-up trampoline (R09 §10.1)}\td{+18\% to +32\% instructions on eval + readback of Init's types; in CPU time +7\% to +18\% without hash-consing and inside the noise with it \verb~|[~meas, 0.3\verb~|]~. The first draft's "8-10\% of the leader's instructions" was an estimate of unmeasured fast paths and is withdrawn. Acceptance for the convention of 8.2: at most +15\% CPU time over plain recursion on Init's and Cedar's declaration types (RECONCILIATION B4)}\td{no stack limit and no 1 GiB thread stacks, exact budgets and rollback by cutting \code{\verb~|W~}, a GC-shallow Java stack, observable steps for differential testing}}
\tr{\td{constants elaborated into code objects}\td{interpret terms with \code{\verb~|(pruned env, expr)~} caches}\td{3-4 µs per elaborated telescope; with the threshold at 10 uses about 15\% of the used constants are elaborated \verb~|[~meas\verb~|]~. Telescope pass: -7\% CPU on Init and -17\% on Std in the first pass when \em{every} used constant is elaborated, -28\% in steady state \verb~|[~meas\verb~|]~}\td{sequential code instead of DAG pointer chasing, static facts, matching of domains, the comparison plan}}
\tr{\td{Truffle JIT on judgment chains}\td{Truffle only for a separate closed-term engine (R08, R09 §1.6)}\td{3.5-18 ms CPU per compiled root for 0-3\% (call-threaded) or 12-23\% (probes inlined) of the pass time of the hot telescopes; on Init every promotion threshold that compiles anything costs more CPU time than never compiling \verb~|[~meas\verb~|]~. On the library corpora it has no target \verb~|[~R13 §5\verb~|]~}\td{one semantics for open and closed terms; an experiment (0.5)}}
}

\p{What the spike does \strong{not} cover: conversion, reduction, binders and tabling. No end-to-end CPU budget for mathlib can be derived from it; the staging gates of section 11 produce those numbers.}
}

\subtree{
\title{0.5 The T2 experiment and its go/no-go number}

\p{Truffle specialises the \em{checking process} only where a judgment chain specialised to a constant is executed often enough to repay its compilation. Measured: a compiled telescope root must run about 400,000 times after promotion to repay 18 ms of compilation at 44 ns saved per run (hot subset, probes inlined) \verb~|[~meas\verb~|]~. \code{\verb~|R13 §5~} concludes that T2 cannot pay on the library corpora under any threshold (upper bound 6\% of cedar's instructions before a compilation cost of half of cedar's total; mathlib's hottest body runs 685 k times, a quarter of that per worker with four workers) and that its only candidates are closed evaluation loops: reachability's five \code{\verb~|decide~} proofs, a handful of con-leche proofs, about 15 \code{\verb~|perf/~} tests, where interning (33-49\% of the instructions) bounds what compiled code can remove. The expectation is therefore \strong{no-go}, and the decision is taken by measurement, once, after staging step 4:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Go} iff, under the arena's limits, total CPU time (all threads, compiler included) is at least \strong{5\% lower} with T2 than with K1 and its case trees on \code{\verb~|reachability.ndjson~}, \strong{or} at least 3\% lower on cedar as a whole; and wall clock is not higher on either; and no corpus of the ladder (Init, Std, the Cedar-level corpora, a mathlib slice) loses more than 0.5\% CPU time.}
\li{\strong{No-go} otherwise: \code{\verb~|rlvm-truffle~} is not built and the Truffle runtime is not linked into the image.}
}

\p{Until then T2 is not part of \code{\verb~|rlvm-machine~}, and no layout, opcode or frame rule in K0/K1 exists for its sake (8.4 keeps the design sketch and the conditions under which it would be sound).}
}
}

